<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sardis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sardis was the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> captured it in 546 BCE after defeating King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, and it became a major Achaemenid administrative centre. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> profile, it was the western terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sardis.jpg" alt="Sardis" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bath-Gymnasium_complex_at_Sardis,_late_2nd_-_early_3rd_century_AD,_Sardis,_Turkey_(17098680002).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sardis, in Lydian Sfar and in Old Persian Sparda, stood in the middle of the Hermus River valley, about 4 kilometres south of the river. Its citadel was built on a steep spur of Mount Tmolus, and a lower town extended to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactolus">Pactolus</a> stream, where archaeologists have found installations for processing alluvial metals. The site was occupied for at least 3,500 years, fluctuating between a wealthy city of international importance and a collection of modest hamlets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sardis was settled before 1500 BCE. In the 7th century BCE it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, whose kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, ruled an empire reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys River</a>. The city covered 108 hectares and was protected by walls twenty metres thick, and three enormous burial tumuli at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bin-tepe">Bin Tepe</a> marked it from a distance. Its artisans specialised in seals and jewellery, and its pottery blended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Anatolian styles.</p><p>After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> at the battles of Pteria and Thymbra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s Persians followed his army back to Sardis and sacked it after a brief siege. The destruction is vivid in the archaeological record: the fortifications burned in a great fire, and arrowheads and household tools lie in the debris, suggesting that civilians joined the fighting. Sardis was rebuilt and, though never again the capital of an independent state, served as the capital of the Persian satrapy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparda">Sparda</a> and as the end station of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a>. Ionian forces burned it in 499 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> received its surrender in 334 BCE. Under Hellenistic rulers the city took on a strongly Greek character, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, hippodrome and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a>, and Antiochos III settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> there. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 129 BCE, was one of the seven churches of Asia, and declined from the 600s after being sacked in 615. It was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> in 1078, retaken in 1097, and probably destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1402; by the 1700s only two small hamlets stood there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site lies near the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarts">Sart</a>, near Salihli in Manisa Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Princeton University excavated it from 1910 to 1914, and the Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, founded in 1958, has since uncovered the Roman bath-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> complex, a synagogue in use from the 4th to the 6th century, Byzantine shops and a Lydian area for refining electrum. It was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2025, for its significance as the home of coinage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sardis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal couriers on the 2,700-kilometre Royal Road from Susa to <b>Sardis</b> could cover the distance in around a week, using relays of fresh riders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ephesus lived from its position at the western end of routes into the Anatolian interior, above all the road to <b>Sardis</b>, capital of the Kingdom of Lydia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek tradition, preserved by Herodotus, held that a dynasty descended from Heracles ruled Lydia for twenty-two generations, about 505 years, which would place its beginnings around 1200 BCE, but this early history is legendary and archaeology at <b>Sardis</b> shows only gradual growth after the collapse of Hittite power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 499 BCE Aristagoras, who governed the city for the tyrant Histiaeus, failed in a Persian-backed attack on Naxos and then led the Ionians in rebellion; with Athenian and Eretrian help the rebels burned <b>Sardis</b> in 498 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydia under Gyges fought them off around c. 665–660 BCE, but in 644 BCE the Cimmerians and the Thracian Treres took <b>Sardis</b>, killed Gyges and went on to destroy Magnesia on the Meander and the Artemision of Ephesus.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 637 BCE the Thracian Treres, allied with the Cimmerians and the Lycians, attacked Lydia and took its capital, <b>Sardis</b>, apart from its citadel, until Scythians under Madyes drove them out of Asia Minor.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
